==Game Speed==
By consuming TF, you can change the game's operating time.

You can choose from 1x, 2x, 3x and [number]x. The last one is added in patch #1.061 to replace Time Warp until Time Warp was fully removed in version #1.068. Currently, [number] can be set to an integer from 4 to 60.<br>
2x consumes 1 second of TF per 1 second of real time. <br>
3x consumes 2 TF per 1 second of real time. In general, nx costs (n-1)s of TF per second of real time.<br>
When the TF runs out, it automatically reverts to 1x.

Increasing game speed reduces the time the game takes to update its state. Automations may work poorly on high game speeds because the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netcode#Tick_rate tickrate] stays constant and instead, the game approximates the result of multiple sequential updates in one game tick.

Because of this, even x2 or x3 speeds will significantly increase score gaining time, especially on early stages, so it is necessary to switch to 1x when decreasing [[Infinity#Challenges|Infinity Challenge]] time.

==Time Warp (REMOVED)==
By consuming TF, you can greatly accelerate the game progress speed for 10 seconds.

After you enter a time to warp in minutes and press the [Time Warp] button, a popup will open for 10 seconds of real time, and the specified time will pass in the game.
 Warp speed = Time(minutes) × 6

For example, if you time warp 10 minutes in 10 seconds, the game will run at x60 speed 

You can not interact with the game process, e.g. buy colors, during time warping. Your [[Automations]] and [[Macro Builder|Macros]] will have to do everything for you.

You can interrupt the time warp at any time by pressing [Stop].

For example, if you start the "60 minutes" time warp above and stop it 5 seconds later, "30 minutes" will have passed in the game.

Therefore, if you want 30 minutes to pass, you can shorten the time by about 5 seconds by specifying "30 minutes" and warping.
Of course, if you miss the stop, you will consume more TF than planned, so be careful.

Please note that if you get an <span class="achievement"> achievement</span> during time warping, you will be unable to press the stop button during the <span class="achievement"> achievement</span> animation

The greater the acceleration, the more unstable your automation will be (making it very inefficient at great speeds), so be careful.<br>
Despite that, on [[Eternity#Dilation|later stages of the game]], it becomes more efficient due to new optimization.

Time Warp was removed on July 10th, 2026 (version #1.068) due to bugs, exploits and progression imbalances and was replaced with Game Speed.

==Offline Flux==
You can convert your TF into OF at a conversion rate of 3:5 (1:00 of TF/h => 1:40 of OF/h).

Offline Flux can only be used when the game is started, and it will try to consume all of your accumulated amount.

It can be interrupted mid-way. Pressing the "Enter Game" button while using it will store the remaining amount and allow you to divide it up for use.

If you uncheck "Auto use at startup", you will be able to choose whether to use it or not. It is generally recommended to leave it unchecked so that it is not used automatically, because the game needs around 20s to return to the right score after startup.

The conditions for use are that you have at least 1 minute of OF, and at least 1 minute in real life has passed since you last quit.

The reason why it is such an inconvenient function is that OF has a higher acquisition rate (1.67 times) compared to TF.

For more details, see the difference between TF and OF.

==Difference between TF and OF==
In summary, TF is easier to use, OF is more efficient and has better storage capacity.

